### Characterization of two-qubit crosstalk

Figure 4 shows the experimental results obtained from implementing the simultaneous HE protocol on _ibmq_lima_. Here, Eq. (18) is used to fit the crosstalk experiment results. Using the previously characterized single-qubit error parameters in the fit, the crosstalk coupling strength \(J\) is found, showing excellent agreement between the model fit and experiment. Note [from Eq. (18)] that the measured qubit's decay rate \(\alpha_{M}\) contains a contribution from the spectator qubit's relaxation rate \(\gamma_{S}\). This agrees with results derived in earlier work, see, for example, Refs. [127, 71, 128].

The noise model is able to accurately predict decay rates \(\alpha_{M}\) with information obtained from single-qubit experiments. This result indicates that, on top of coherent rotations, the presence of \(ZZ\) crosstalk causes an additional incoherent mixed dissipation between the qubits. Under the assumption that \(J_{\text{MS}}\gg\gamma_{S}\), the dynamics are well described by Eq. (18). However, as noted in Ref. [127], when \(J_{\text{MS}}\sim\gamma_{S}\), this incoherent evolution arises from the ensemble average of stochastic coherent errors on a per-trajectory basis, rather than from a white noise spectrum. This observation provides additional evidence of the detrimental effect that crosstalk has on applications relying on simultaneous qubit manipulation and preservation. This is particularly interesting when considering error suppression techniques such as DD, which can only cancel the coherent rotations but not the incoherent contributions [32, 71].

### Time-dependent correlated noise on the IBMQP

In addition to spatially correlated errors such as crosstalk, clear evidence of strong time-correlated noise on IBMQP processors is observed. This type of noise is ubiquitous in solid-state devices, and is known in particular to be widely present in transmon-based qubits [77, 78, 79, 80, 129, 130, 131]. As described in Sec. II.4, properties of time-correlated stochastic noise are captured by the mean and PSD \(S(\omega)\) of the noise. The FFF is the core framework used to investigate the effect of time-correlated errors on system dynamics. The FFF quantifies the noisy dynamics by computing an overlap integral in frequency space between the noise PSD and the FFs \(F(\omega,\tau)\), which capture the sensitivity of the system. Furthermore, the FFF can be used to reconstruct spectral properties of the noise via QNS; see Appendix B.1 for a detailed description of the FFF and QNS. In this section, we show that through selected characterization experiments, namely FTTPS and its variations, time-correlated non-Markovian dephasing

