As a result, the rotated frame noise propagator is

\[\tilde{U}(\tau) = \mathcal{T}_{+}e^{-i\int_{0}^{\tau}dt\hat{H}_{\text{X}}(t)} \tag{101}\] \[\stackrel{{(1)}}{{\approx}} e^{-i\frac{\beta}{2}\int_{0}^{\tau}dt\left(\sigma_{z}\cos(\theta\frac{t}{ \delta t})+\sigma_{y}\sin(\theta\frac{t}{\delta t})\right)},\]

where \(\mathcal{T}_{+}\) is the time ordering operator and in (1) we have approximated the time-ordered dynamics by the first-order Magnus expansion. More specifically, for a single \(X\) gate, where \(\tau=\delta t\), we obtain

\[\tilde{U}(\delta t) \approx e^{-i\frac{\beta t}{\delta t}\left(\sigma_{z}\sin\theta+ \sigma_{y}(1-\cos\theta)\right)} \tag{102}\] \[\approx e^{-i\frac{\beta t}{\delta t}\sigma_{y}},\]

where we used the weak noise conditions \(\epsilon\ll 1\) and \(\beta\delta t\ll 1\) in approximating \(\sin\theta\approx-\pi\epsilon\), \(1-\cos\theta\approx 2-(\pi\epsilon)^{2}/2\), and \(\beta/\theta\approx\beta/\pi\) to first order.

In the lab frame, the noisy \(X\) gate can be written in terms of the control and toggling frame propagators as

\[X^{\prime} = U_{C}(\delta t)\tilde{U}(\delta t) \tag{103}\] \[\approx Xe^{-i\frac{\pi\epsilon}{2}\sigma_{x}}e^{-i\frac{\beta t }{\pi}\sigma_{y}}.\]

Consequently, the noisy implementation of the building block \(G\) becomes

\[G^{\prime} = X^{\prime}ZX^{\prime}Z \tag{104}\] \[\approx Xe^{-i\frac{\pi\epsilon}{2}\sigma_{x}}e^{-i\frac{\beta t }{\delta t}\sigma_{y}}ZXe^{-i\frac{\beta t}{2}\sigma_{x}}e^{-i\frac{\beta t}{ \delta t}\sigma_{y}}Z\] \[= e^{-i\frac{\pi\epsilon}{2}\sigma_{x}}Xe^{-i\frac{\beta t}{ \delta t}\sigma_{y}}XZe^{-i\frac{\beta t}{2}\sigma_{x}}ZZe^{-i\frac{\beta t}{ \delta t}\sigma_{y}}Z\] \[= e^{-i\frac{\pi\epsilon}{2}\sigma_{x}}e^{i\frac{\beta t}{\delta t }\sigma_{y}}e^{i\frac{\beta t}{2}\sigma_{x}}e^{i\frac{\beta t}{\delta t} \sigma_{y}}\] \[\approx e^{j\frac{\beta t}{\delta t}\sigma_{y}}e^{j\frac{\beta t}{ \delta t}\sigma_{y}}\] \[\approx e^{j\frac{\beta t}{\delta t}\sigma_{y}},\]

where we used that \(X\) and \(Z\) anticommute, as well as \(e^{-i\frac{\pi\epsilon}{2}\sigma_{x}}\) and \(e^{-i\frac{\beta t}{\delta t}\sigma_{y}}\) commute to first order; operator equalities hold up to a global phase. Additionally, we used \(\sigma_{j}\,e^{-i\phi\sigma_{2}}\sigma_{j}=e^{-i\phi\sigma_{y}\sigma_{2}\sigma_ {y}}=e^{i\phi\sigma_{x}}\) for \(k\neq j\). This result indicates that the interleaved \(Z\) gates cancel the \(X\) over-rotation errors given by \(\epsilon\), but maintain the first-order detuning errors, thus causing FPW errors.

Finally, the full intended operation consists of \(d\) repetitions of the \(G^{\prime}\) operator, yielding

\[(G^{\prime})^{d}\approx e^{2d\frac{\beta t}{\pi}\sigma_{y}}. \tag{105}\]

The survival probability for \(\tau=2d\delta t\) can then be computed as

\[p\left(\tau\right) = \left|\left\langle 0\right|\left(G^{\prime}\right)^{d}\left|0\right\rangle \right|^{2} \tag{106}\] \[\approx \cos\left(\frac{2\beta}{\pi}\tau\right)^{2},\]

from where the functional form of Eq. (17) becomes clear. However, the numerical factor of the oscillation frequency of \(2\beta/\pi\) depends on the specific shape of the pulse. Interestingly, the difference between the square pulse and the Gaussian pulse is a factor of \(2/3\), as discussed in Sec. III.2. Lastly, the decay rate can be found by successive implementation of Eq. (102), and is given by \((\mu+\eta)/2=3/4\gamma+\lambda/2+v\).

### _g. (XT) crosstalk_

The (XT) circuits can be computed similarly to the Ramsey experiments earlier in this section. In fact, the calculation is analogous, by renaming qubit \(Q\) to \(A\) and promoting the TLS-to-qubit \(B\). The latter involves considering a detuning \(\beta_{B}\) for qubit \(B\), as well as dissipative error contributions. Thus, the crosstalk Hamiltonian becomes,

\[H_{\text{XT}}=\frac{\beta_{A}}{2}\sigma_{z}^{A}+\frac{\beta_{B}}{2}\sigma_{z} ^{B}+\frac{J}{2}\sigma_{z}^{A}\sigma_{z}^{B}. \tag{107}\]

Like in the TLS case, \(H_{\text{XT}}\) is diagonal, and so is the time propagator it induces. More specifically, \(U_{\text{XT}}(t)=e^{-iH_{\text{XT}}t}\), where the diagonal entries correspond to \(e^{-i\frac{t}{2}(\beta_{A}+\beta_{B}+J)}\), \(e^{-i\frac{t}{2}(\beta_{A}-\beta_{B}-J)}\), \(e^{-i\frac{t}{2}(-\beta_{A}+\beta_{B}-J)}\), \(e^{-i\frac{t}{2}(-\beta_{A}-\beta_{B}+J)}\). Both qubits are initialized with \(\sqrt{X}\) gates, allowed to evolve freely for a time \(\tau/2\) after which an \(X\) gate is applied to both qubits. Then, the qubits evolve freely once again, before two final \(\sqrt{X}\) gates are applied prior to measurement in the computational basis. The resulting \(z\) component of the Bloch sphere after tracing out qubit \(B\) is given by

\[v^{XT}(\tau)=e^{-\left(\frac{\gamma_{A}+\gamma_{B}}{2}+\lambda_{A}\right)\tau} \left(\cos(J\tau)+\frac{\gamma_{B}}{2J}\sin(J\tau)\right) \tag{108}\]

to lowest order in the dissipative noise parameters.

## Appendix B Qns

Time-correlated noise is commonly observed in superconducting qubits [32, 68, 79, 86, 148, 149]. Its presence is commonly detected via superexponential decay in \(T_{2}\) experiments and more precisely characterized via QNS [89, 98]. In the case of the IBMQP devices, we find a substantial number of devices where qubits exhibit time-correlated dephasing and control noise. Below, we discuss the FFF, a mathematical framework used to investigate the effect of correlated noise, and QNS based on FTTPS. We briefly summarize FTTPS-based QNS in the presence of dephasing and multiplicative control noise.

### **1. Filter function formalism**

The FFF takes a frequency domain perspective on the effect of spatiotemporal noise on a quantum system. Here, we provide an overview for the FFF focusing on a single qubit governed by the noise Hamiltonian \(H_{N,1}(t)\)[Eq. (10)] and control Hamiltonian \(H_{C}(t)\) given by Eq. (3), where \(\Theta(t)=\int_{0}^{t}\Omega\left(s\right)ds\). In the toggling frame, the density matrix is transformed by \(\tilde{\rho}(t)=U_{C}(t)\rho(t)U_{C}^{\dagger}(t)\), and the noise Hamiltonian becomes

\[\tilde{H}_{N}(t) =U_{C}(t)H_{N}(t)U_{C}^{\dagger}(t)\] \[=\left[\cos\Theta(t)\sigma_{z}+\sin\Theta(t)\sigma_{y}\right] \beta(t)+\Omega\left(t\right)\epsilon(t)\frac{\sigma_{x}}{2}.\]

The time propagator of the noise dynamics in the toggling frame is given by

\[\tilde{U}_{N}(t) =\mathcal{T}_{+}\exp\left(\int_{0}^{t}ds\tilde{H}_{N}(s)\right)\] \[\approx\exp\left(-i\tilde{a}(\tau)\cdot\tilde{\sigma}\right), \tag{11}\]

where the approximation is a result of employing the Magnus expansion, truncating the dynamics to first order, and introducing the so-called error vector \(\tilde{a}(t)=\int_{0}^{t}ds\left[\frac{1}{2}\Omega(s)\epsilon(s),\sin\Theta( s)\beta(s),\cos\Theta(s)\beta(s)\right]\)[96, 150]. The full dynamics are generated by \(U(t)=U_{C}(t)\tilde{U}_{N}(t)\).

In anticipation of the dynamics generated during an FTTPS circuit, we will focus on the noise-averaged survival probability \(p(\tau)=\langle\left(-y|U(\tau)|-y\right)\rangle_{\epsilon,\beta}\), where \(|-y\rangle=1/\sqrt{2}(|0\rangle-i\,|1\rangle)\) and \(\tau\) is the total time of one sequence within the FTTPS protocol. Accounting for the fact that the sequences generate identity evolution, the probability can be simplified further as

\[p(\tau) =\left\langle\cos^{2}a(\tau)+\left(a_{y}^{2}(\tau)/a(\tau)\right) \sin^{2}a(\tau)\right\rangle_{\epsilon,\beta}\] \[=\frac{1}{2}\left(1+\left\langle\frac{a_{y}^{2}(\tau)+\left[a_{x }^{2}(\tau)+a_{z}^{2}(\tau)\right]\cos(2a^{2}(\tau))}{a^{2}(\tau)}\right\rangle _{\epsilon,\beta}\right)\] \[\overset{(1)}{=}\frac{1}{2}\left(1+e^{-\chi(\tau)}\cos\zeta( \tau)\right), \tag{12}\]

where \(a(\tau)=|\tilde{a}(\tau)|\) and the \(\langle\cdot\rangle_{\epsilon,\beta}\) represents the average over noise realizations. The expression in (1) results from assuming the pulses are instantaneous and thus, \(a_{y}(\tau)=0\). The dynamics are characterized by a decay factor \(\chi(\tau)\equiv\left\langle a^{2}(\tau)\right\rangle_{\epsilon,\beta}\) and rotation angle \(\zeta(\tau)\equiv 2\left\langle a(\tau)\right\rangle_{\epsilon,\beta}\).

Commonly, these expressions are transformed to the frequency domain, as this can provide greater intuition that draws on classical signal processing concepts. Expressing the error vector components in the frequency domain, we obtain

\[\left\langle a_{x}^{2}(\tau)\right\rangle_{\epsilon,\beta} =\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\infty}F_{\epsilon}(\omega,\tau)S_{ \epsilon}(\omega)d\omega, \tag{13}\] \[\left\langle a_{z}^{2}(\tau)\right\rangle_{\epsilon,\beta} =\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\infty}F_{\beta}(\omega,\tau)S_{\beta}( \omega)d\omega,\]

where the control and dephasing FFs are defined by

\[F_{\epsilon}(\omega,\tau) =\frac{1}{4}\left|\int_{0}^{\tau}\Omega\left(t\right)e^{i\omega t }\right|^{2}, \tag{14}\] \[F_{\beta}(\omega,\tau) =\left|\int_{0}^{\tau}\cos\Theta(t)e^{i\omega t}\right|^{2}.\]

The PSDs are defined in Sec. II.4.

## 2 Dephasing QNS with FTTPS

In order to extract information about a device's dephasing noise PSD, we leverage the FTTPS. The advantage of using FTTPS for QNS lies in the large spectral concentration of their FFs [57]. This feature results in a favorable condition number in the FF matrix and thus, reduces the chance of encountering an ill-posed inversion problem in the spectrum reconstruction procedure. Here, we will study FTTPS in the absence of control noise.

Starting from Eq. (12) and eliminating the contribution from control noise, \(\zeta(\tau)=2\tilde{\beta}\tau\) while

\[\chi(\tau)=\frac{1}{\pi}\int_{0}^{\infty}S_{\beta}(\omega)F_{\beta}(\omega, \tau)d\omega. \tag{15}\]

In the large \(\tau\) limit, the FF of the \(k\)th FTTPS \(F_{\beta,k}(\omega,\tau)\) is well approximated by

\[F_{\beta,k}(\omega,\tau)\approx\tau^{2}\delta(\omega-2\pi\,k/\tau), \tag{16}\]

where \(\omega_{k}=2\pi\,k/\tau\) [see Fig. 11(a)]. The total FTTPS circuit duration is \(\tau=2K\delta t\). Using Eq. (15), it is straightforward to estimate the PSD by computing \(\chi(\tau)\approx\tau S(\omega_{k})\), where to approximate the integral we used explicitly the discretized frequency step \(\delta\omega=2\pi/\tau\). Note that estimates of \(\chi\) are obtained from experimentally determined \(p_{y,k}(\tau)\).

Figure 11: Fixed total-time pulse sequences filter functions: (a) dephasing and (b) control. (c) Robust-fixed total-time pulse sequences control filter functions. \(K=64,\delta t=0.035\)\(\mu\)s. Note that the fixed total-time pulse sequences control filter functions have large support in low frequencies, whereas the robust-fixed total-time pulse sequences control filter functions do not.

## 3 FTTPS in the presence of stochastic control noise

Another type of correlated noise was observed via FTTPS: correlated control noise. Imperfect control can manifest via fluctuating fields in the control lines, and can be stochastic or coherent. In the case of stochastic noise, a common approach is to treat the noise fluctuations as white, uncorrelated noise. In the case of \(X\) control, white noise can be well represented by a bit-flip channel (see Appendix D). In the general case, stochastic fluctuations of the control fields can be correlated in time, requiring a more sophisticated modeling approach. In this appendix, we focus on evaluating the effect of stochastic control noise, specifically on the FTTPS.

Consider the survival probability in Eq. (20) under the influence of strong control noise, such that the dephasing noise can be neglected. The probability oscillates with an angle \(\zeta(\tau)=\tilde{\epsilon}\,\Theta(\tau)\), while the decay is dictated by \(\chi(\tau)=\left\{a_{\mathrm{z}}^{2}(\tau)\right\}_{\epsilon,\beta}\). Under the square pulse approximation and assuming the pulses are located at times \(t=\tau_{n}\approx(\tau/2k)(n-1/2)\), the mean of the accumulated angular error is \(\xi_{k}=\pi k\tilde{\epsilon}\). The control FFs (ctrl-FFs) are defined as

\[F_{\epsilon,k}(\omega)=\left|\frac{\pi}{2}\sum_{n=1}^{2k}e^{-i\omega\tau_{k}} \right|^{2}\approx\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\frac{\sin(\omega\tau/2)}{\sin(\omega\tau k /4)}\right)^{2}, \tag{21}\]

where the approximation comes from assuming the instantaneous pulse limit. Roughly approximating the ctrl-FFs by \(F_{\epsilon,k}(\omega)=4k^{2}\delta(\omega)\) [see Fig. 11(b)], we obtain an expression for the second moment \(\chi_{\epsilon,k}(\tau)\approx\sigma k^{2}\) (defined in Sec. IV.3.3), with \(\sigma=\pi^{2}\delta\omega S_{\epsilon}(0)\). Thus, for predominantly low-frequency noise, \(S_{\epsilon}(0)\) captures the relevant noise strength.

### R-Fttps

To obtain further confirmation of the presence of correlated control noise, we define the Robust-FTTPS (R-FTTPS) sequences, by alternating the sign of every other \(X\) pulse in the standard FTTPS, i.e., \(X\rightarrow-X\). In practice, this is implemented by appending virtual \(Z\) gates on each side of even pulses. In the absence of control errors, this sign change is undetectable. However, in their presence, the sign change implies \(e^{-\frac{i}{2}\Theta(i\sigma_{x}}\to e^{+\frac{i}{2}\Theta(i\sigma_{x}}\). Alternating signs will suppress the coherent and low-frequency contributions of the noise, as can be seen by computing the R-FTTPS accumulated phase: \(\xi_{k}^{R}=\pi\tilde{\epsilon}\,\sum_{n=1}^{2k}(-1)^{n}=0\). The second moment is now characterized by the R-FTTPS ctrl-FFs

\[F_{\epsilon,k}^{R}(\omega)=\left|\frac{\pi}{2}\sum_{n=1}^{2k}(-1)^{n}e^{-i \omega\tau_{n}}\right|^{2}\approx\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\frac{\sin(\omega\tau/2)}{ \cos(\omega\tau k/4)}\right)^{2} \tag{22}\]

[see Fig. 11(c)].

## Appendix C Gaussian vs square pulses in FPW experiments

Remarkably, we find in practice that most experimental phenomena observed can be well described within the constant pulse approximation. This model robustness to pulse-shape greatly simplifies the task of simulating driven dynamics. The LME solution can be used whenever \(X,\sqrt{X}\) appear in a circuit of interest without the need of any Trotterization. One notable exception to this convenient simplification can be found in the FPW experiments. Since FPW experiments aim to amplify gate errors, it is perhaps not surprising that the results are largely influenced by pulse-shaping effects. In this appendix, we analyze the differences between Gaussian and constant pulses in FPW experiments.

The result of FPW experiments under Markovian noise can be parametrized with a decay rate \(a\) and oscillation frequency \(b\) as \(f(t;a,b)=e^{-at}\cos(bt)\). In the constant pulse case, it can be shown analytically that \(a_{\mathrm{const}}=\frac{3}{4}\gamma+(\lambda+\nu)/2,b_{\mathrm{const}}=(2/\pi )\,\beta\). On the other hand, when the qubit is driven with a Gaussian pulse, it is challenging to find fully analytical expressions for the survival probability of the FPW experiments in the presence of noise.

A Gaussian pulse is characterized by \(\Omega(t)=Ae^{-(t-\delta t/2)^{2}/2\sigma^{2}}+B\) for \(t\in[0,\delta t)\), where the \(B\) parameter is chosen such that the pulse starts and ends at zero, i.e., \(\Omega(0)=\Omega\left(\delta t\right)=0\). A ubiquitous choice for the width of the Gaussian is \(\sigma=\delta t/4\)[114, 151], while \(A\) is chosen to produce the desired rotation angle \(\int_{0}^{\delta t}\Omega\left(t\right)dt=\theta\). Under these conditions, we simulate the dynamics using a discretization of \(N=160\) steps, consistent with IBM pulse characteristics. The simulation is performed by numerically solving the LME [Eq. (11)], with \(X\) rotation

Figure 12: Noisy simulation of finite pulse-width circuits for constant (blue) and Gaussian pulses (orange). (a) Simulation for \(\beta=0.5\) MHz (crosses) and fit (solid lines) using \(f(t;a=a_{\mathrm{const}},b)=e^{-at}\cos(bt)\). The decay rates are set to \(\gamma=0.05\) MHz, \(\lambda=v=0.01\) MHz. (b) Oscillation frequency parameter \(b\) as a function of detuning \(\beta\), obtained from fitting \(f(t;a=a_{\mathrm{const}})\) to simulations. This shows that for a constant pulse, \(v^{\prime\prime}(t)\) oscillates with a frequency \(2\beta/\pi\), whereas for a Gaussian pulse it oscillates with \(4\beta/3\pi\).

angles given by \(\theta_{n}=\Omega\left(n\delta t\right)\delta t/N\), for \(n=0,\ldots,N-1\). In Fig. 12, we show numerically that fitting \(f(t;a,b)\) to the simulation results of FPW experiments under Gaussian pulses yields the same decay rate as in the constant pulse case, i.e., \(a_{\text{Gauss}}=a_{\text{const}}\), and an oscillation frequency \(b_{\text{Gauss}}=\left(4/3\pi\right)\beta=\frac{3}{2}b_{\text{const}}\). This can be interpreted as Gaussian pulses providing suppression with a factor of \(2/3\) against detuning processes. Since the experiments are implemented with Gaussian pulses, in order to account for this suppression, we modify the analytical fit expression with a factor of \(2/3\), i.e., \(v(t)=e^{-\tau\left(\frac{3}{2}\tau+\frac{2+\pi}{2}\right)}\cos(\left(4/3\pi \right)\beta\tau)\), as presented in Eq. (17).

## Appendix D Relating Stochastic Noise With Error Channels

In this appendix, we show how some common error channels, namely PD and depolarization, can be related to stochastic noise processes with simple statistical properties.

### Dephasing noise and PD

Here, we establish a Hamiltonian description of the PD quantum channel. We start with the time-dependent dephasing Hamiltonian given in Eq. (10) with \(\epsilon(t)\equiv 0\). The noise-averaged state of the system after time \(\tau\) is

\[\mathcal{E}(\rho) =\langle U(\tau)\rho U^{\dagger}(T)\rangle_{\beta}\] \[=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\ 0&e^{-i\int_{0}^{t}\beta(t)dt}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{00}&\rho_{01} \\ \rho_{10}&\rho_{11}\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\ 0&e^{i\int_{0}^{t}\beta(t)dt}\end{pmatrix}\rangle_{\beta}\] \[=\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{00}&\rho_{01}e^{i\int_{0}^{t}\beta(t)dt}\\ \rho_{10}e^{-i\int_{0}^{t}\beta(t)dt}&\rho_{11}\end{pmatrix}\rangle_{\beta}\] \[\approx\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{00}&\rho_{01}e^{i\tilde{\beta}\tau- \chi(\tau)}\\ \rho_{01}e^{-i\tilde{\beta}\tau-\chi(\tau)}&\rho_{11}\end{pmatrix}, \tag{20}\]

where \(\chi\) is given by Eq. (35). The decaying component of the off-diagonal elements become dependent on the control in general. However, if the noise is white, i.e., \(S(\omega)=S_{0}\), it is straightforward to show (by following the steps in Appendix B.1) that \(\chi(\tau)=S_{0}\tau\). This indicates that the superoperator formalism is equivalent to the time-dependent Hamiltonian description when the noise is white.

More concretely, the PD superoperator \(\mathcal{E}_{PD}(\rho)\) can be written in terms of the Krauss operators \(E_{0}=\sqrt{1-p}I,E_{1}=\sqrt{p}Z\) with damping rate \(p\). The effect on a general state \(\rho\) is

\[\mathcal{E}_{PD}(\rho) =E_{0}\rho E_{0}+E_{1}\rho E_{1}\] \[=\left(1-\frac{p}{2}\right)\rho+\frac{p}{2}Z\rho Z\] \[=\left(1-\frac{p}{2}\right)\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{00}&\rho_{01}\\ \rho_{10}&\rho_{11}\end{pmatrix}+\frac{p}{2}\left(\begin{matrix}\rho_{00}&- \rho_{01}\\ -\rho_{10}&\rho_{11}\end{matrix}\right)\] \[=\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{00}&\rho_{01}(1-p)\\ \rho_{10}(1-p)&\rho_{11}\end{pmatrix} \tag{21}\]

and can be interpreted as acting on the state \(\rho\) with a \(Z\) operator with a given probability \(p\). Adding a detuning noise \(\bar{\beta}\sigma_{z}\) with propagator \(U_{\beta}(t)=e^{-i\bar{\beta}\sigma_{z}/2}\) leads to a density matrix

\[U_{\beta}(t)\mathcal{E}_{PD}(\rho)U_{\beta}^{\dagger}(t)\] \[=\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{00}&\rho_{01}e^{-i\bar{\beta}t}(1-p)\\ \rho_{10}e^{i\bar{\beta}t}(1-p)&\rho_{11}\end{pmatrix}, \tag{22}\]

from where the identification of the detuning parameter with the mean of \(\beta(t)\) becomes evident. Lastly, by identifying \(p=1-e^{-S_{0}\tau}\) it becomes clear that the PD channel can be interpreted as the white noise limit of a time-dependent dephasing Hamiltonian, where the dephasing time can be defined as \(T_{2}=1/S_{0}\).

### Isotropic uncorrelated noise and depolarization

The control noise case discussed in Sec. IV.3.3 is entirely analogous to the dephasing case. Rather, to showcase the versatility of the stochastic Hamiltonian description, we turn to depolarizing noise. In the superoperator formalism, the Kraus operators for depolarizing noise are \(E=\{\sqrt{1-3p/4}I,\sqrt{p}X/2,\sqrt{p}Y/2,\sqrt{p}Z/2\}\), which can be interpreted as \(\rho\) is left alone with probability of \(1-p\), and the operators \(X,Y,Z\) being applied with probability \(p/3\). The effect of depolarization on a general state can be written as

\[\mathcal{E}_{D}(\rho) =p\,\frac{I}{2}+(1-p)\rho\] \[=\begin{pmatrix}\rho_{00}(1-p)+p/2&\rho_{01}(1-p)\\ \rho_{10}(1-p)&\rho_{11}(1-p)+p/2\end{pmatrix}. \tag{23}\]

Next, we propose a general time-dependent Hamiltonian, and find the conditions that the parameters need to satisfy in order for the stochastic Hamiltonian to reproduce the effects of depolarizing noise. We write a Hamiltonian \(H_{N}(t)=\vec{\eta}(t)\cdot\vec{\sigma}\), where we consider \(\vec{\eta}(t)\) a Gaussian and stationary random variable with zero mean. The time evolution propagator is

\[U(\tau) =e^{-i\int_{0}^{\tau}\vec{\eta}(t)dt\cdot\vec{\sigma}}=e^{-i\vec{ \theta}_{\tau}\vec{\sigma}}\] \[=I\cos\theta_{\eta}-i(\hat{\pi}_{\eta}\cdot\vec{\sigma})\sin \theta_{\eta}, \tag{24}\]where we defined \(\vec{\theta}_{\eta}=\int_{0}^{\tau}\vec{\eta}(t)dt=\theta_{\eta}\hat{n}_{\eta}\), with \(\hat{n}_{\eta}^{i}=\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^{i}/\theta_{\eta}\) a unit vector. The state becomes

\[\mathcal{E}(\rho) =\langle U(\tau)\rho U^{\dagger}(\tau)\rangle_{\eta}\] \[=\rho\left\langle\cos^{2}\theta_{\eta}\right\rangle_{\eta}-i[ \left\langle\cos\theta_{\eta}\sin\theta_{\eta}\hat{n}_{\eta}\right\rangle_{ \eta}\cdot\vec{\sigma},\rho]\] \[\quad+\left\langle(\hat{n}_{\eta}\cdot\vec{\sigma})\rho\left( \hat{n}_{\eta}\cdot\vec{\sigma}\right)\sin^{2}\theta_{\eta}\right\rangle_{ \eta}. \tag{100}\]

In the weak noise approximation, the first two terms simplify greatly. Dropping the subscript \(\eta\) from the noise averaging, these terms can be expressed as

\[\left\langle\cos^{2}\theta_{\eta}\right\rangle\approx \left\langle\left(1-\frac{1}{2}\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right)^{2}\right\rangle =1-\left\langle\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right\rangle, \tag{101}\] \[\left\langle\cos\theta_{\eta}\sin\theta_{\eta}\hat{n}_{\eta}\right\rangle\approx \left\langle\left(1-\frac{1}{2}\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right)\theta_{ \eta}\frac{\vec{\theta}_{\eta}}{\theta_{\eta}}\right\rangle=\left\langle\vec{ \theta}_{\eta}\right\rangle=0. \tag{102}\]

Next, we make two additional assumptions regarding the statistical properties of the noise parameters: (1) we assume that the noise is uncorrelated along different axes and (2) the noise is isotropic, i.e.,

\[\left\langle\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^{i}\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^{i}\right\rangle =\left\langle\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^{i}\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^{i}\right\rangle \delta_{\vec{\eta}}, \tag{103}\] \[\left\langle\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^{i}\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^{i}\right\rangle =\frac{\left\langle\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right\rangle}{3}, \tag{104}\]

respectively. With these simplifying assumptions, the last term in Eq. (100) becomes

\[\left\langle(\hat{n}_{\eta}\cdot\vec{\sigma})\rho\left(\hat{n}_{\eta}\cdot \vec{\sigma}\right)\sin^{2}\theta_{\eta}\right\rangle\approx\sum_{ij}\left\langle \hat{n}_{\eta}^{i}\hat{n}_{\eta}^{j}\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right\rangle\sigma_{i} \rho\sigma_{j}.\]

This can be further simplified as

\[\sum_{ij}\left\langle\hat{n}_{\eta}^{i}\hat{n}_{\eta}^{j}\theta_ {\eta}^{2}\right\rangle\sigma_{i}\rho\sigma_{j} =\sum_{ij}\left\langle\frac{\partial_{\eta}^{i}\partial_{\eta}^ {j}}{\theta_{\eta}^{2}}\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right\rangle\sigma_{i}\rho\sigma_{j}\] \[=\sum_{ij}\left\langle\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^{i}\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^ {j}\right\rangle\sigma_{i}\rho\sigma_{j}\] \[=\sum_{i}\left\langle\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^{i}\vec{\theta}_{\eta}^ {i}\right\rangle\sigma_{i}\rho\sigma_{i}=\frac{\left\langle\theta_{\eta}^{2} \right\rangle}{3}\sum_{i}\sigma_{i}\rho\sigma_{i}\] \[=\frac{\left\langle\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right\rangle}{3}\left(2I- \rho\right). \tag{105}\]

Thus,

\[\mathcal{E}(\rho) =\rho\left(1-\left\langle\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right\rangle\right)+ \frac{\left\langle\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right\rangle}{3}\left(2I-\rho\right)\] \[=\frac{2}{3}\left\langle\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right\rangle I+\left(1- \frac{4}{3}\left\langle\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right\rangle\right)\rho. \tag{106}\]

From here, the depolarizing error can be recovered by setting

\[p=\frac{4}{3}\left\langle\theta_{\eta}^{2}\right\rangle=\frac{4}{3}\sum_{i} \int_{0}^{\tau}\int_{0}^{\tau}dtdt^{\prime}\left\langle\eta_{i}(t)\eta_{i}(t^ {\prime})\right\rangle. \tag{107}\]

Analogously to the dephasing case, a constant \(p\) implies that noise must be uncorrelated and isotropic in the Hamiltonian description. In this case, if \(\left\langle\gamma_{i}(t)\gamma_{i}(t^{\prime})\right\rangle=C_{\eta}\delta(t- \dot{\tau})\), we get \(p=4C_{\eta}\dot{\tau}/3\).

## Appendix E Evidence of Correlated Dephasing Through Dd

In Sec. IV, we explore the detection and modeling of correlated dephasing noise. By adding a number of DD pulses on T2 experiments, dephasing can be detected by comparing the relative performance. Figure 13 shows experimental results obtained from running an increasing number of DD pulses on qubit 6 of _ibmq_guadalupe_, as function of total evolution time \(\tau\).

It is clear that the performance improves with the number of DD pulses. Based on the theory of DD, if this improvement is due to increased filtering of dephasing noise, the decay rate should decrease as \(1/d\), where \(d\) is the number of DD pulses. We fit the function \(p_{D}(\tau)=(1+e^{-\gamma_{d}(\tau)})/2\), where \(\chi_{d}(\tau)\approx\int S(\omega)\operatorname{sinc}((\omega-\omega_{d}) \tau)^{2}d\omega\) is the overlap function. Note that the number of DD pulses shifts the center of the frequency response, given by \(\omega_{d}=d\,2\pi/\tau\). Therefore, we can fit the experimental results to a function \(\chi_{d}(\tau)=A/d+B\), where the parameters \(A,B\) are common to all DD experiments. The results of these fits are presented as solid lines with excellent agreement, obtaining further confirmation of the presence of correlated dephasing noise.

Figure 13: Experimental results (circles) obtained from running an increasing number of dynamical decoupling pulses on qubit 6 of _ibmq_guadalupe_. Improved performance with the number of dynamical decoupling pulses is observed. The experiment results are fit to a function \(\chi_{d}(\tau)=A/d+B\), where the parameters \(A,B\) are common to all dynamical decoupling experiments. The results of these fits are presented as solid lines, where we obtain \(A=0.0063(2)\) and \(B\approx 0\) (within error bars).

## Appendix F Parametrizing the Markovian Rb Decay Rate

We perform numerical studies to examine the impact of Markovian errors on the RB decay rate \(r_{\mathrm{RB}}\). In the absence of SPAM errors, the RB experimental result can be modeled as \(p_{\mathrm{RB}}(L)=(1+e^{-r_{\mathrm{RB}}L})/2\), where \(L\) is the number of Cliffords in a given RB sequences. We perform simulation of multiple combinations of the Markovian parameters and find that, to first order, the decay rate can be approximated as

\[r_{\mathrm{RB}}\approx\delta t(\gamma+\lambda+\upsilon)+\left(\frac{\pi\, \epsilon}{2}\right)^{2}. \tag{10}\]

Thus, the impact of dissipative Markovian errors on the decay rate increases linear as a function of the time elapsed in the sequence. Coherent errors, on the other hand, enter quadratically. Note that these results are consistent with the literature; see, for example, Ref. [152]. Figure 14 presents examples of simulations performed with Markovian parameters near values obtained from real devices. The noise parameters were chosen to be "weak" by default, while varying one parameter set as "strong" (see left and right columns of Table 3, respectively). The exponential decays (solid lines) are analytically computed by combining Eq. (10) and \(p_{\mathrm{RB}}(L)\).

## Appendix G RB Standard Deviations

To complement the analysis in Sec. IV.1, we compare the variances obtained from the RB experiments on 13 different qubits of _ibm_algiers_ (qubits 0, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 14, 17, 22 and 24), where the Markovian model with low-frequency dephasing noise was found to provide a good description of the qubit's dynamics. For each of these qubits, we follow the steps outlined in Sec. III.1 to fit the noise parameters and simulate the RB experiments. Then, for each RB Clifford length \(L=2^{i}\) for \(i\in[3,10]\), we compute the standard deviations \(\sigma_{\mathrm{exp}},\sigma_{\mathrm{sim}}\) for the experiment and simulations, respectively. The results are shown collectively in Fig. 15 in a scatter plot to visualize the degree of correlation. The Pearson correlation coefficient was computed between the two datasets and was found to be 0.70. This strong correlation provides evidence that the model is able to capture the RB statistics to a larger extent than just the mean alone. Deviations from the perfect correlation (shown as the solid line in Fig. 15) may be due to low statistics, as only 10 RB sequences per length were run, and model deviations. Although promising, this showcases that a more thorough analysis could shed additional light into the origin of these discrepancies. Due to the complexity of running additional experiments on the IBMQP, this is left for future work.

## Appendix H Resonances in Fttps

As discussed in Sec. IV.3.2, low-frequency dephasing noise is prominent in the superconducting qubit devices examined in this study. Despite the common spectral features of low-frequency noise accompanied by a white noise floor, exceptions do exist. In particular, we find a subset of qubits that exhibit resonance peaks at high frequency. An example of this phenomenon can be seen in Fig. 16, detected in an FTTPS experiment.

The large resonance at \(k=30\) can be mapped to a frequency value by drawing upon the FFF (see Appendix B.1). The \(k\)th FF \(F_{k}(\omega)\) has a narrow frequency sensitivity peak centered at \(\omega=2\pi\,k/\tau\) [see Fig. 11(a)], where \(\tau=2K\delta t\) is the FTTPS duration. Consequently, the resonance observed in Fig. 16 corresponds to a frequency of \(\approx\)41 \(\pm\) 1 MHz. This frequency value is consistent with that observed in other qubits, typically in the range of 25-70 MHz. A possible origin of this phenomenon is frequency collisions with neighboring qubits, since IBMQP Eagle generation devices have typical resonance frequency differences between nearest-neighbor qubits in the order of

